Let $R$ be a commutative unital ring, let $q,\rho\in R^\times$, put $z=q-q^{-1}$, and choose $\delta\in R$ with $z(\delta-1)=\rho-\rho^{-1}$. For $n\ge2$, the BMW algebra $BMW_n(R;q,\rho,\delta)$ is the unital associative $R$-algebra generated by invertible $g_i$ and elements $e_i$ for $1\le i<n$, subject to the following relations (an adjacent relation is imposed only when both indices exist):

- braid and distant braid: $g_i g_{i+1}g_i=g_{i+1}g_i g_{i+1}$ and $g_i g_j=g_jg_i$ for $|i-j|\ge2$;
- distant cups and caps: $g_i e_j=e_jg_i$ and $e_i e_j=e_j e_i$ for $|i-j|\ge2$;
- adjacent cup-cap: $e_i e_{i\pm1}e_i=e_i$, $g_i g_{i\pm1}e_i=e_{i\pm1}e_i$, and $e_i g_{i\pm1}g_i=e_i e_{i\pm1}$;
- Kauffman skein and untwisting: $g_i-g_i^{-1}=z(1-e_i)$, $g_i e_i=e_i g_i=\rho^{-1}e_i$, and $e_i g_{i\pm1}e_i=\rho e_i$;
- loop: $e_i^2=\delta e_i$.

For the boundary ranks, set $BMW_0(R;q,\rho,\delta)=BMW_1(R;q,\rho,\delta)=R$, with the empty and single straight tangle as their respective units.

If $z$ is invertible, the parameter relation reads $\delta=1+(\rho-\rho^{-1})/z$. If $z$ is not invertible, the presentation uses the universal coefficient relation $z(\delta-1)=\rho-\rho^{-1}$ and does not divide by $z$. The quotient by the two-sided ideal generated by all $e_i$ is the cup-cap-zero Hecke quotient; this definition does not assert a BMW diagram-basis theorem or a BMW quantum-centralizer theorem.

Let $A$ be an indeterminate and let $\mathbb Z[A^{\pm1}]$ be the Laurent
polynomial ring in $A$. A **nonempty unoriented regular link diagram** is the
finite planar projection and over/under data of a regular oriented link diagram
after its strand arrows are forgotten. Its finite crossing set is denoted
$C(D)$, and at least one closed component is required. The empty link diagram is
outside this definition.

At a crossing use the following local chart: the over-strand runs from the
southwest endpoint to the northeast endpoint and is drawn continuously over the
under-strand, which runs from northwest to southeast with a gap at the
intersection. In this chart the **A-smoothing** joins the two top endpoints and
joins the two bottom endpoints; the **B-smoothing** joins the two left endpoints
and joins the two right endpoints. These labels are attached to this displayed
chart and are transported by orientation-preserving changes of local
coordinates.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.72, line cap=round, line join=round]
  \begin{scope}
    \draw[thick] (-1,1)--(-0.14,0.14);
    \draw[thick] (0.14,-0.14)--(1,-1);
    \draw[very thick] (-1,-1)--(1,1);
    \node at (0,-1.45) {crossing};
  \end{scope}
  \begin{scope}[xshift=3.4cm]
    \draw[thick] (-1,1) .. controls (-0.35,0.45) and (0.35,0.45) .. (1,1);
    \draw[thick] (-1,-1) .. controls (-0.35,-0.45) and (0.35,-0.45) .. (1,-1);
    \node at (0,-1.45) {$A$-smoothing, weight $A$};
  \end{scope}
  \begin{scope}[xshift=6.8cm]
    \draw[thick] (-1,1) .. controls (-0.45,0.35) and (-0.45,-0.35) .. (-1,-1);
    \draw[thick] (1,1) .. controls (0.45,0.35) and (0.45,-0.35) .. (1,-1);
    \node at (0,-1.45) {$B$-smoothing, weight $A^{-1}$};
  \end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
```

For a state $S:C(D)\to\{A,B\}$, let $a(S)$ and $b(S)$ be the numbers of
A- and B-smoothings, and let $|S|$ be the number of closed circles in the
crossingless diagram obtained by performing all smoothings. Set
$$\delta:=-A^2-A^{-2}$$
and define the **Kauffman bracket** of $D$ by
$$\langle D\rangle:=\sum_{S:C(D)\to\{A,B\}} A^{a(S)-b(S)}\delta^{|S|-1}\in\mathbb Z[A^{\pm1}].$$
Because $D$ is nonempty, every complete smoothing has $|S|\ge1$, so the loop
exponent is nonnegative. A crossingless $k$-component unlink with $k\ge1$ has
bracket $\delta^{k-1}$.

